From VZARTARIAN at usgs.gov  Mon Feb  1 17:03:59 1999
From: VZARTARIAN at usgs.gov (Valerie Zartarian)
Date: Mon, 01 Feb 1999 12:03:59 -0500
Subject: [S] condensing a dataframe sequentially
Message-ID: <s6b5983c.061@IGSRN008.er.usgs.gov>

Last week I posed the following problem:

My original dataframe VZ.df looks like:
V1          V2            V3            V4          V5
a       b       d       g       5
a       b       d       g       5
a        b      d       g       5
a        b      d       g       5
a       c       e       g       5
a       c       e       h       5
a       c       e       g       5
a       c       e       g       5
a       c       e       h       5
a       c       e       h       5
a       c       f       g       5
a       c        f      g       5

I want to aggregate V5 over common rows sequentially as follows:

a       b       d       g       20
a       c       e       g       5
a       c       e       h       5
a       c       e       g       10
a       c       e       h       10
a       c       f       g       10

SOLUTION A: by Bill Dunlap (bill at statsci.com) 

> seqAggregate.data.frame( VZ.df$V5,by=VZ.df[1:4],sum)

where seqAggregate.data.frame is aggregate.data.frame with the following lines 
added:

*** 12,17 ****
--- 12,21 ----
                by <- list(by)
                names(by) <- nm
        }
+ # add sequential group id to by
+       r <- rle(do.call("paste", c(list(sep = "\002"), lapply(by, function(x)
+       as.character(x)))))
+       by$GroupSeqID <- rep(seq(along = r$lengths), r$lengths)
        if(is.null(names(by)))
                names(by) <- paste("Group", seq(along = by))
        val <- lapply(x, tapply, by, FUN, ..., simplify = F)

SOLUTION B: by Christian Keller <ckeller at aicos.com>

ii <- codes(interaction(VZ.df[,1:4], drop=T))
ii <- c(ii[1],ii)
jj <- cumsum(diff(ii)!=0)

VZ.df$jj <- jj
aggregate(VZ.df$V5, by=VZ.df[,c("V1","V2","V3","V4","jj")],
FUN=sum)[,-5]

I tried Solution A and it worked fine when I applied it to a minute-by-minute 
dataframe of 5 columns and 1440 rows.  However, when I applied it to my actual 
data frame which is 5 columns and 17280 rows (corresponding to a row every 5 
seconds of a day) I got an error message that the object size was 5.8E6, 
exceeding the default of 5E6.  So, I set options(object.size = 5E7) and ran the 
function again.  This hung up my computer (400 MHz 12 GB PC) both times I tried 
it, so I'm wondering how I can free up memory or make the program more 
efficient from a memory standpoint.  Any suggestions?   Also, would Solution B 
be more memory efficient?

Thanks in advance for your suggestions.

Valerie

