From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Thu Feb  4 07:45:24 1999
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Thu, 04 Feb 1999 07:45:24 +0000
Subject: Re: [S] NMDS: Sammon
In-Reply-To: <36B8B6D2.337BD939@gis.umn.edu>
References: <36B8B6D2.337BD939@gis.umn.edu>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.9902040735180.25528-100000@auk.stats>

On Wed, 3 Feb 1999, Steve Friedman wrote:

> 
> Greetings,
> 
> I'm trying to examine the association of species along environmental
> gradients using the sammon function (MASS, library).  To do this
> I have constructed a matrix, partially shown below.   The data
> is interpreted as follows, each column represents the total number
> of individuals, by species (column heading) for the plot.  In this case
> the
> plots are General Land Office records where the maximum number of
> individuals is constrainted to be four.    When I run the function
> though I
> get an error message indicating that records 8 and 12 have a zero or
> negative
> distance.  I've read the help files for this function, and am still at
> loss for what is
> expected for input.

Have you read MASS though, especially p.386?  Sammon mapping is not defined
for datasets with one or more pairs of points that are identical and so
have zero distance. Funnily enough, there is an example of that
in MASS.  The help file says explicitly

REQUIRED ARGUMENTS:
d:     distance structure of the form  returned  by  dist,  or  a
       full,   symmetric   matrix.    Data   are  assumed  to  be
       dissimilarities  or  relative  distances,  but   must   be
       positive except for self-distance.

You supplied d with zero values other than for self-distance, I gather.

I can't really tell you what to do, as you tell us too little about your
aims. You could either drop one of the rows 8 and 12, or you could use
another form of MDS. Your message seems to suggest there are 9280
observations: if correct, you are going to find MDS slow and hard to
interpret.

As a general point, when a library is produced to support a book or paper,
it is expected that you read the documentation it supports, and the
introduction to this group asks that questions about third-party software
are directed to the author in the first instance.

