From fharrell at virginia.edu  Mon Feb  8 13:56:35 1999
From: fharrell at virginia.edu (Frank E Harrell Jr)
Date: Mon, 08 Feb 1999 08:56:35 -0500
Subject: Re: [S] Isotonic regression
Message-ID: <001501be536a$d7233480$5a6c8f80@fharrell.biostat.virginia.edu>

A different approach can be found in Rob Tibshirani's "Bootstrap Bumping" 
method.
Here you fit models from say 100 resamples of (X,Y) and select the subset of
fits that turn out to be monotonic.  This works if you put suitable 
restrictions on
the function (e.g., not too many knots in a regression spline).  You can even 
get
a "poor person's" posterior probability of monotonicity from this approach, by
computing the proportion of resamples yielding monotonic fits.  Bootstrap 
bumping
is very easy to implement in S-Plus.

See http://www-stat.stanford.edu/~tibs

-Frank Harrell

