Hi Ziv,

It was my understanding that there were still some problems with frailty
models; I guess I am missing something important?  

However, while waiting for survival5, there is a set of programs, "The
survival kit (v. 3.0)" by Ducrocq & Casella, which is freely available on
the Web (http://www.boku.ac.at/nuwi/popgen/softskit.htm).  These programs
will fit frailty models [Cox or Weibull], with time dependent variables.
Two references are: 
Ducrocq, V., Casella, G. (1996) A Bayesian analysis of mixed survival
models. Genet. Sel. Evol., 28 : 505-529.
Ducrocq, V. and Svlkner J. (1998b) +The Survival Kit - V3.0 ;, a package
for large analyses of survival data. In : 6th World Cong. on Genet. Appl.
Livest. Prod., January 11-16, University of New-England, Armidale, Australia.
(some of the papers are available from the Web site above).

Another related approach is in Sorensen, Gianola & Korsgaard (Acta Agric.
Scand., '98, 48: 222-229) [they deal with Bayesian mixed-effects models for
censored normal data].   

Some other alternatives (in my case for the analysis of repeated cross-over
designs with right-censored data) are the models of Wei & collaborators
(e.g., JASA, '89, 84: 1065-1073; Stats. in Medicine, '94, 13: 2233-2247;
Stats. in Medicine, '97, 16: 833-839)., which (I believe) can be fitted in
SPlus using the "cluster" option in coxph.  

Ramon

In addtion, if you are intersting in SAS macros to estimate with frailty
models you can look at http://www.biostat.mcw.edu:80/devlop/softmenu.html.
