The QDUNNETT function allows unequal cell sizes for different treatment
groups therefore
is more general than QMVT, which requires euqal correlation among its
components.  But
the correlation coefficients among the estimates of treatment differences
in the QDUNNETT
setting still satisfy these constraints,
rho_ij = (1/n_0) / sqrt(1/n_i+1/n_0) / sqrt(1/n_j+1/n_0)

The online help for QDUNNETT says that "The function obtains the critical
point by numerical
integration and a secant method."  The core computation is carried out in a
Fortran subroutine.

What I do not know is whether the numeric method the Fortran routine uses
depends on the rho_ij
constraints?  The QDUNNETT procedure seems to be able to fit a more general
setting if the
constraints are not needed to calculate the critical value.

Your help is greatly appreciated.

Jeen Liu

